he you home affairs ministers of welcome  progress made in key areas to improve  the fight against illegal immigration  the minister's urged all stakeholders to  continue to actively implement  recommendations to combat the problem  made by the so-called taskforce  Mediterranean the task force was set up  last October after almost 300 migrants  died when their overcrowded boat sank  off the coast of lamb producer in Italy  the tragic events in Lampedusa are still  very much vivid in our minds and we need  to implement the actions identified to  further prevent loss of lives and build  up a credible migratory policy for the  future the aim is to boost cooperation  with countries that people leave and  transit to reach Europe improve legal  avenues for migrants to come to the EU  beef up the fight against trafficking  and organized crime strengthen border  surveillance and help member states  under high migration pressures The  ministers asked the European Commission  to provide a progress report in June  data protection was also discussed with  justice and Home Affairs ministers  briefed on proposals to protect people  with new rules on the processing of  personal information and on the  protection of privacy when that  information is transferred to countries  outside the EU the rules aim to provide  consistent and high level data  protection as well as to improve  cooperation and trust between the police  and justice authorities of different  member states
 

